          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why do some categories need a fourth or fifth character while others do not?",
          "answer": "Most ICD-10-CM categories need more characters because the three-character category is too broad to record the details a clinician needs. The extra characters add specificity.\n\n**What the extra characters add.** The three-character form is the category. Characters four and five add detail such as cause, anatomical site or severity. Character six often records laterality (right, left or unspecified), and a seventh character, where a category uses one, records the encounter, such as initial treatment, subsequent treatment or sequela for injuries. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\n**Examples from the retrieved guidance:**\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11)** is never used at three characters. The fourth and later characters name the complication, such as kidney, eye, nerve or circulation involvement. Diabetes with no complications has its own code ending in 9, which is why E11.9 is often quoted. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- **COPD (J44)** uses its fourth character to record whether a complication is present at the encounter: with acute lower respiratory infection, with acute exacerbation, or without either. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n\n**Categories that stand alone.** Essential hypertension (I10) is a complete code at three characters. The guidance says most categories require a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is billable, and I10 takes none. Its category covers the various wordings for high blood pressure. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\n**What the sources do not cover.** The passages describe what the characters mean and give examples. They do not set out the full rule for each category, so they do not explain exactly why one category was built with subdivisions and another was not. For the required character count for a specific category, check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list or ask a coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
